```go
type Client struct {
    timeout  time.Duration
    retries  int
    baseURL  string
    useTLS   bool
    maxConns int
}

type Option func(*Client)

func WithTimeout(d time.Duration) Option      { return func(c *Client) { c.timeout = d } }
func WithRetries(n int) Option                { return func(c *Client) { c.retries = n } }
func WithTLS(enabled bool) Option             { return func(c *Client) { c.useTLS = enabled } }
func WithMaxConns(n int) Option               { return func(c *Client) { c.maxConns = n } }

func NewClient(baseURL string, opts ...Option) *Client {
    c := &Client{
        baseURL:  baseURL,
        timeout:  30 * time.Second,
        retries:  3,
        maxConns: 10,
    }
    for _, opt := range opts {
        opt(c)
    }
    return c
}
```

A five-parameter constructor where several parameters share a type (`int`, `int`, ... `bool` ...) is error-prone at the call site — `NewClient(5, 30, "...", true, 10)` is easy to get subtly wrong (swap `timeout` and `retries`) with no compiler warning, and every caller has to specify every parameter even to change just one. The functional-options version makes each setting self-documenting at the call site (`NewClient(url, WithRetries(5))`), gives every setting a sensible default, and lets new options be added later without breaking existing callers. The `timeout` parameter's type also changed from a bare `int` (ambiguous — seconds? milliseconds?) to `time.Duration`, which makes the unit part of the type instead of a comment.

