Printing an error message and returning a zero value hides the failure from the caller — code calling `Divide(a, 0)` sees `0`, indistinguishable from a legitimate result of `0`, and has no way to programmatically detect or handle the failure. Returning an explicit `error` lets the caller decide how to react (retry, abort, log, substitute a default) instead of that decision being made — silently — inside `Divide` itself.

