func LoggingInterceptor(logger *log.Logger) grpc.UnaryServerInterceptor {
    return func(
        ctx context.Context,
        req interface{},
        info *grpc.UnaryServerInfo,
        handler grpc.UnaryHandler,
    ) (interface{}, error) {
        start := time.Now()

        resp, err := handler(ctx, req)

        code := codes.OK
        if err != nil {
            code = status.Code(err)
        }
        logger.Printf("method=%s duration=%s code=%s", info.FullMethod, time.Since(start), code)

        return resp, err
    }
}

// registered with:
// grpc.NewServer(grpc.UnaryInterceptor(LoggingInterceptor(logger)))
```

An interceptor wraps every unary RPC on the server, so this is the right layer for cross-cutting concerns like logging, auth, or metrics rather than repeating the same code in every handler. Calling `handler(ctx, req)` first and logging afterward means the logged duration and status code reflect the actual outcome of the wrapped call, including any error it returned.

`defer` schedules a call to run when the enclosing function returns, which makes it ideal for cleanup that must happen regardless of which return path is taken: closing files, unlocking mutexes, closing HTTP response bodies. Arguments to a deferred call are evaluated immediately, not when the deferred call runs, which trips people up in loops:

```go
// bug: every deferred call closes the SAME (last) file
for _, name := range names {
    f, _ := os.Open(name)
    defer f.Close()
}

// fix: wrap the loop body so defer fires each iteration
for _, name := range names {
    func() {
        f, _ := os.Open(name)
        defer f.Close()
        process(f)
    }()
}
```

