Never close a channel from a receiver, and never close a channel from more than one goroutine — both cause a panic (`close of closed channel` or a send on a closed channel from a goroutine that hasn't noticed yet). The rule that avoids both problems: only the single goroutine that owns a channel — typically whichever goroutine is the sole sender — should close it, and it should do so only after every other potential sender has already finished, usually coordinated with a `sync.WaitGroup`:

```go
var wg sync.WaitGroup
out := make(chan int)

for _, w := range workers {
    wg.Add(1)
    go func(w Worker) {
        defer wg.Done()
        out <- w.Result()
    }(w)
}

go func() {
    wg.Wait()
    close(out) // safe: runs only after every sender is done
}()
```

```go
func ProcessOrdered[T, R any](ctx context.Context, items []T, workers int, fn func(T) (R, error)) ([]R, error) {
    results := make([]R, len(items))
    errs := make([]error, len(items))

    sem := make(chan struct{}, workers)
    var wg sync.WaitGroup

    for i, item := range items {
        i, item := i, item
        wg.Add(1)
        sem <- struct{}{}
        go func() {
            defer wg.Done()
            defer func() { <-sem }()
            results[i], errs[i] = fn(item)
        }()
    }

    wg.Wait()

    for _, err := range errs {
        if err != nil {
            return nil, err
        }
    }
    return results, nil
}
```

Writing directly into `results[i]` (rather than sending results down a channel and reassembling them) keeps output order matching input order for free, since each goroutine owns a distinct index and there's no data race — every goroutine writes to a different slice element. The semaphore channel caps how many goroutines run `fn` concurrently without limiting how many are launched, which is fine for CPU/memory-bounded work but means all `len(items)` goroutines exist at once, just mostly blocked; for very large `items`, prefer a worker-pool structure that only launches `workers` goroutines total.

