```go
type Optional[T any] struct {
    value T
    ok    bool
}

func Some[T any](v T) Optional[T] {
    return Optional[T]{value: v, ok: true}
}

func None[T any]() Optional[T] {
    return Optional[T]{}
}

func (o Optional[T]) Get() (T, bool) {
    return o.value, o.ok
}

func (o Optional[T]) OrElse(fallback T) T {
    if o.ok {
        return o.value
    }
    return fallback
}
```

In practice, Go code almost always reaches for a plain `(T, bool)` return (matching the built-in map-lookup idiom, `v, ok := m[key]`) or a `*T` when absence needs to be distinguishable from a zero value — both are more idiomatic and don't require readers to learn a new type. An `Optional[T]` wrapper mainly earns its place when a struct field itself needs to represent "present vs absent" (where a bare `(T, bool)` can't be a field), and even then, a pointer field is usually simpler unless you specifically want to avoid pointer semantics.

```go
type Result[T any] struct {
    value T
    err   error
}

func Ok[T any](v T) Result[T] {
    return Result[T]{value: v}
}

func Err[T any](err error) Result[T] {
    return Result[T]{err: err}
}

func (r Result[T]) Unwrap() (T, error) {
    return r.value, r.err
}

func (r Result[T]) IsOk() bool {
    return r.err == nil
}

func MapResult[T, R any](r Result[T], f func(T) R) Result[R] {
    if r.err != nil {
        return Err[R](r.err)
    }
    return Ok(f(r.value))
}

// usage:
// r := parseAge("25")          // Result[int]
// doubled := MapResult(r, func(n int) int { return n * 2 })
// v, err := doubled.Unwrap()
```

This is worth noting as a pattern you'll see referenced, but it goes against the grain of standard Go, where `(T, error)` multi-return is the idiomatic way to signal fallibility and every Go developer already knows how to check it. A `Result[T]` type adds a layer of indirection (`Unwrap()`) that Go's own tooling, error-wrapping conventions (`%w`), and most libraries aren't built around — reach for it deliberately, not as a default, and expect it to feel foreign to Go code review.

```go
func process(ctx context.Context, jobs <-chan int, workers int) <-chan int {
    results := make(chan int)
    var wg sync.WaitGroup

    for i := 0; i < workers; i++ {
        wg.Add(1)
        go func() {
            defer wg.Done()
            for {
                select {
                case <-ctx.Done():
                    return
                case job, ok := <-jobs:
                    if !ok {
                        return
                    }
                    select {
                    case results <- job * job:
                    case <-ctx.Done():
                        return
                    }
                }
            }
        }()
    }

    go func() {
        wg.Wait()
        close(results)
    }()

    return results
}
```
Each worker reads jobs, sends results, and watches `ctx.Done()` on both channel operations so it never blocks forever. A separate goroutine waits for all workers to finish before closing `results`, which is what lets a downstream `for range results` loop terminate cleanly.

