The standard library's `slices` package (Go 1.21+) covers common cases like sorting and searching — `slices.Sort`, `slices.Contains`, `slices.Index` — but doesn't include Map/Filter/Reduce, so writing small generic helpers like these for your own codebase is still common and idiomatic where the pattern repeats.

```go
type RateLimiter struct {
    mu         sync.Mutex
    tokens     float64
    maxTokens  float64
    refillRate float64 // tokens per second
    lastRefill time.Time
}

func NewRateLimiter(maxTokens, refillRate float64) *RateLimiter {
    return &RateLimiter{
        tokens:     maxTokens,
        maxTokens:  maxTokens,
        refillRate: refillRate,
        lastRefill: time.Now(),
    }
}

func (r *RateLimiter) Allow() bool {
    r.mu.Lock()
    defer r.mu.Unlock()

    now := time.Now()
    elapsed := now.Sub(r.lastRefill).Seconds()
    r.tokens = min(r.maxTokens, r.tokens+elapsed*r.refillRate)
    r.lastRefill = now

    if r.tokens < 1 {
        return false
    }
    r.tokens--
    return true
}
```

This refills lazily on each `Allow` call based on elapsed wall-clock time, rather than running a background ticker goroutine — simpler to reason about and nothing to leak or shut down. For production use under real load, `golang.org/x/time/rate` implements the same idea with better precision and is worth reaching for instead of hand-rolling this.

Go 1.20+ added `errors.Join`, which combines several errors into one that `errors.Is`/`errors.As` can still unwrap and match against individually:

```go
func ValidateUser(u User) error {
    var errs []error

    if u.Name == "" {
        errs = append(errs, errors.New("name is required"))
    }
    if !strings.Contains(u.Email, "@") {
        errs = append(errs, errors.New("email is invalid"))
    }
    if u.Age < 0 {
        errs = append(errs, errors.New("age cannot be negative"))
    }

    return errors.Join(errs...)
}
```

`errors.Join(errs...)` returns `nil` if `errs` is empty (or contains only nil errors), so this composes cleanly with the usual `if err := ValidateUser(u); err != nil` check at the call site — no separate "were there any errors" check needed. Its `Error()` output joins each message on its own line, and `errors.Is`/`errors.As` search through all joined errors, not just the first.

```go
type LRUCache[K comparable, V any] struct {
    mu       sync.Mutex
    capacity int
    items    map[K]*list.Element
    order    *list.List
}

type entry[K comparable, V any] struct {
    key   K
    value V
}

func NewLRUCache[K comparable, V any](capacity int) *LRUCache[K, V] {
    return &LRUCache[K, V]{
        capacity: capacity,
        items:    make(map[K]*list.Element, capacity),
        order:    list.New(),
    }
}

func (c *LRUCache[K, V]) Get(key K) (V, bool) {
    c.mu.Lock()
    defer c.mu.Unlock()

    var zero V
    el, ok := c.items[key]
    if !ok {
        return zero, false
    }
    c.order.MoveToFront(el)
    return el.Value.(*entry[K, V]).value, true
}

func (c *LRUCache[K, V]) Put(key K, value V) {
    c.mu.Lock()
    defer c.mu.Unlock()

    if el, ok := c.items[key]; ok {
        el.Value.(*entry[K, V]).value = value
        c.order.MoveToFront(el)
        return
    }

    if c.order.Len() >= c.capacity {
        oldest := c.order.Back()
        if oldest != nil {
            c.order.Remove(oldest)
            delete(c.items, oldest.Value.(*entry[K, V]).key)
        }
    }

    el := c.order.PushFront(&entry[K, V]{key: key, value: value})
    c.items[key] = el
}
```

`container/list` gives O(1) move-to-front and eviction from the back, and the `map[K]*list.Element` gives O(1) lookup — the two together make both `Get` and `Put` O(1). The generic `[K comparable, V any]` parameterization means the same cache implementation works for any key/value types without duplicating the logic per type.

