The original hand-rolls request routing with nested `if`/`else`, which gets unreadable fast as routes are added and duplicates logic (method checking, 404/405 handling) that `http.ServeMux` already provides. Go 1.22's `ServeMux` supports method-prefixed patterns (`"GET /health"`) directly, so the router itself handles wrong-method (405-equivalent, via its own not-found/method-not-allowed behavior) and unmatched-path cases — deeply nested conditionals for routing are a strong signal to reach for the standard router (or a third-party one) instead of hand-writing dispatch logic.

